## ABSTRACT

This dissertation develops a mathematically rigorous, architecturally grounded, and strategically complete theory of the firm in the age of artificial intelligence. We formalize the Learning Company — an organization that continuously converts human experience into compounding machine intelligence through a structured, self-reinforcing loop between two novel capital forms: Human Capital (HC) and Token Capital (TC). The dissertation makes seven original theoretical contributions. First, we prove that HC and TC are strict formal complements under a Cobb-Douglas production function with nonlinear coupling, establishing that AI capability growth systematically increases — not decreases — the marginal value of human expertise. Second, we derive the full optimal control problem governing the Learning Company, introducing a structured feedback flow F_t as an explicit third control variable alongside HC and TC investment, capturing the annotation, elicitation, and distillation bottleneck that is the binding constraint on most real-world learning loops. We derive closed-form optimality conditions including the optimal feedback intensity rule, the shadow price equations for all three capital stocks, and the steady-state allocation under power-law costs. Third, we prove that the Organizational Learning Loop (OLL) converges to a unique stable fixed point under bounded stochastic perturbations using Banach contraction machinery combined with stochastic Gronwall analysis. Fourth, we formalize the knowledge conversion channel as a Shannon information-theoretic model and prove the maximum rate at which tacit human knowledge can be converted into machine-learnable intelligence. Fifth, we specify a five-layer Decentralized Learning Loop Architecture (DLLA) in which organizational coherence emerges from local agent incentives rather than a centralized planner, and prove deadlock-freedom and convergence via a potential game argument. Sixth, we analyze the sustainability of the learning advantage under strategic imitation, deriving a closed-form sustainability condition and characterizing the optimal defense investment against competitor reverse-engineering. Seventh, we prove that the Nash equilibrium of the competitive game is the universal Learning Company equilibrium and that the Foundation Model Monopoly is dynamically unstable. We close with formal societal implications, a minimal viable empirical measurement framework, and an explicit treatment of algorithmic bias as a formal depreciation risk.

**Keywords:** Bipartite Cognitive Capital, Organizational Learning Loop, Token Capital, Feedback Cost, Decentralized MARL, Knowledge Conversion Channel, Competitive Imitation Defense, Frontier Ecosystem, Nash Equilibrium, Potential Game

