## CHAPTER 7: COMPETITIVE EQUILIBRIUM AND STRATEGIC IMITATION

### 7.1 The Competitive Market Model

Consider an industry with N firms. At time t, let K_t^{LC} firms be Learning Companies (LC) operating the DLLA, and N − K_t^{LC} be Static Companies (SC) using generic AI tools without proprietary capital accumulation. Firms compete on output price P(Y_total) where Y_total = Σ_i Y_i.

### 7.2 The Compounding Intelligence Differential

**Theorem 7.1 (Diverging CID).** Let firm A be an LC starting at BCC_A(0) = (H₀, T₀ > 0) and firm B an SC starting at (H₀, 0). With equal total investment I per period:

CID(t) = Y_A(t) − Y_B(t) satisfies lim_{t→∞} CID(t)/e^{(μ_A − μ_B)t} = c > 0  ... (7.1)

where μ_A − μ_B ≥ β_T Ψ(H₀, T₀, F*) · (β/T₀) + [κν/(H₀+T₀)] · (Ḣ₀ + Ṫ₀) > 0.

**Proof.**

The log-output growth rates are:

μ_A = d𝔼[ln Y_A]/dt = α · 𝔼[Ḣ_A/H_A] + β · 𝔼[Ṫ_A/T_A] + ν · κ · 𝔼[∂lnΓ/∂H · Ḣ_A + ∂lnΓ/∂T · Ṫ_A]

μ_B = d𝔼[ln Y_B]/dt = α · 𝔼[Ḣ_B/H_B]

The difference has three contributions:

(a) Token capital growth term: β · 𝔼[Ṫ_A/T_A] > 0 since Ṫ_A > 0 (LC develops TC) while SC has T_B ≡ 0.

(b) Coupling amplification of H growth: Under the OLL, 𝔼[Ḣ_A/H_A] > 𝔼[Ḣ_B/H_B] even at equal I_H, because H_A benefits from the coupling term β_H Φ(T_A, H_A, F_A*) > 0 while Φ(0, H_B, F) = 0 (by Proposition 2.1(vi), the learning-from-AI function vanishes when T = 0).

(c) Γ coupling term: Since T_A > 0 and growing, ∂lnΓ/∂T · Ṫ_A > 0 adds a strictly positive third contribution to μ_A.

Therefore μ_A > μ_B with difference bounded below by the stated expression. The CID grows exponentially: Y_A(t)/Y_B(t) ≥ e^{(μ_A − μ_B)t} → ∞, so CID(t) → ∞ as claimed. □

**Corollary 7.1 (First-Mover Perpetuity).** Among Learning Companies, firm A starting the OLL at time t₀ < t₁ maintains a permanent advantage over firm B starting at t₁. The advantage gap grows at the OLL growth rate μ_A and cannot be closed by equal-investment late entry.

### 7.3 Nash Equilibrium of the LC Game

**Theorem 7.2 (Universal LC Equilibrium).** In the N-firm competitive game, for discount rate ρ > 0 and LC transition cost c_LC, there exists a critical threshold ρ̄ such that for ρ < ρ̄, the unique Nash Equilibrium is universal Learning Company adoption. The threshold is:

ρ̄ = (μ_A − μ_B) · Y_avg / c_LC  ... (7.2)

and for typical knowledge-industry parameters (μ_A − μ_B ~ 15%, Y_avg/c_LC ~ 5–10x), ρ̄ ~ 75–150% per year, far exceeding typical corporate discount rates of 8–15%.

**Proof.** Consider firm j's deviation from SC to LC. The net present value of the deviation is:

ΔNPV_j = ∫₀^∞ e^{-ρt} [P · CID_j(t)] dt − c_LC

= ∫₀^∞ e^{-ρt} [P · c · e^{(μ_A − μ_B)t}] dt − c_LC (by Theorem 7.1)

= P · c · ∫₀^∞ e^{-(ρ − μ_A + μ_B)t} dt − c_LC

= P · c / (ρ − μ_A + μ_B) − c_LC

This is positive if and only if ρ < μ_A − μ_B + P·c/c_LC = ρ̄. When ρ < ρ̄, the deviation is profitable for every firm regardless of the strategies of others — the LC strategy is a dominant strategy. Therefore the unique Nash Equilibrium is universal LC adoption. □

### 7.4 Foundation Model Monopoly Instability

**Theorem 7.3 (FMM Instability).** A market structure in which all firms are pure AI consumers (T_i^{prop} ≡ 0 for all i) is not a Nash Equilibrium. There always exists a profitable deviation to LC status for at least one firm.

**Proof.** Under the pure consumer market, all firms have identical effective TC = T^{gen}_FMM (the foundation model provider's generic capability). Competition drives operational profits to zero (Bertrand logic on homogeneous capability). Any firm j deviating to LC status gains CID_j(t) > 0 (Theorem 7.1) and earns positive rents. The deviation is profitable. Since this applies symmetrically to every firm, the pure consumer equilibrium has no Nash equilibrium incumbent — it is dynamically unstable under rational expectations. □

### 7.5 Strategic Imitation and the Defense of Competitive Advantage

A fundamental challenge to the analysis is that a learning firm's advantage could be eroded if competitors observe its outputs and reverse-engineer its token capital. We now analyze this threat formally, extending the model to a duopoly setting with imitation.

**Definition 7.1 (Imitation Dynamics).** Consider a duopoly {A, B} where A is the LC incumbent and B is an imitating competitor. Firm B can allocate imitation investment I_t^{imit} that increases its Token Capital by exploiting observable outputs of A:

dT_t^B = [α_T I_t^{TC,B} + β_T Ψ(H_t^B, T_t^B, F_t^B) − δ_T T_t^B + η(M_t^A) · ω(T_t^A, T_t^B) · I_t^{imit}] dt + σ_T dW_t^{(T),B}  ... (7.3)

where:
- η(M_t^A) ∈ [0, η_max] is the imitation efficiency, decreasing in A's defensive moat investment M_t^A.
- ω(T_t^A, T_t^B) ∈ [0,1] is the knowledge proximity function capturing how similar the two firms' token capitals are (imitation is more effective when the knowledge bases are close).

**Definition 7.2 (Defensive Moat Investment).** Firm A invests M_t^A ≥ 0 in defensive moats (differential privacy of training data, proprietary knowledge graph schemas, legal IP protections, evaluation framework obfuscation) at cost c_M · M_t^A. The imitation efficiency satisfies:

η(M_t^A) = η_0 · e^{−θ_M M_t^A}, θ_M > 0  ... (7.4)

capturing exponential decay of imitation effectiveness as moat investment rises.

**Theorem 7.4 (Sustainability of Advantage — General Form).** In the duopoly {A, B}, Firm A maintains a persistent Token Capital lead T_t^A > T_t^B for all t > t_0 if and only if, at the steady-state moat investment M^*:

β_T Ψ(H^A, T^A, F^{A,*}) − δ_T > η_0 · e^{−θ_M M^*} · ω(T^A, T^B)  ... (7.5)

The left-hand side is A's organic learning rate (net of depreciation). The right-hand side is B's imitation rate at steady-state moat level M^*.

**Proof.**

**Step 1: Formulate the differential game.** Firm A maximizes total discounted profits net of moat costs. Firm B maximizes profits from imitation. The system state is (T_t^A − T_t^B) ≡ Δ_t (the lead). Δ_t evolves as:

dΔ_t = [β_T Ψ^A − δ_T T^A − (α_T I^{TC,B} + β_T Ψ^B − δ_T T^B + η(M^A)ω·I^{imit})] dt + noise

= [β_T(Ψ^A − Ψ^B) − δ_T Δ_t − η(M^A)ω·I^{imit}] dt + noise  ... (7.6)

**Step 2: Steady-state lead condition.** At the interior equilibrium where B is investing optimally in imitation (∂Π^B/∂I^{imit} = 0) and A is investing optimally in moats (∂Π^A/∂M^A = 0):

B's optimal imitation investment: I^{imit,*} = (η(M^A) · ω · λ_T^B) / c_imit  ... (7.7)

A's optimal moat investment: θ_M · M^A · (−η_0 e^{-θ_M M^A} · ω · I^{imit,*}) · λ_T^A = c_M → M^* = (1/θ_M) · ln[η_0 · ω · θ_M · I^{imit,*} · λ_T^A / c_M]  ... (7.8)

**Step 3: Persistence of Δ.** The lead Δ_t is persistent (Δ_t → Δ_∞ > 0) if and only if the drift of Δ in (7.6) is positive at Δ = 0:

[β_T(Ψ^A − Ψ^B)|_{Δ=0} − η_0 e^{-θ_M M^*} · ω · I^{imit,*}] > 0

Since Ψ^A(H^A, T^A, F^{A,*}) > Ψ^B(H^B, T^B, F^{B,*}) when A has more human capital and better feedback (a consequence of the first-mover advantage Corollary 7.1), the first term is positive. The condition (7.5) is exactly this: the LC's organic learning rate must exceed the imitator's effective imitation rate after moat protection. □

**Corollary 7.2 (Optimal Moat Investment).** At the optimal M^*:

M^* = (1/θ_M) · ln(η_0 · ω · θ_M · λ_T^A / (c_M · c_imit))  ... (7.9)

M^* is increasing in η_0 (harder to defend against more efficient imitators) and decreasing in c_M (moats are cheaper to build). Critically, M^* is also decreasing in ω(T_t^A, T_t^B) — the less similar the two firms' knowledge bases, the less moat investment is needed. This provides a formal justification for knowledge differentiation strategy: building TC in specialized domains that are distant from competitors' knowledge bases reduces the knowledge proximity ω, lowering imitation effectiveness even before moat investment.

**Corollary 7.3 (Simultaneous Moat Equilibrium in N-Firm Game).** In the N-firm game where each firm can imitate any other, the condition for persistence of LC Firm A's advantage against all simultaneous imitators is:

β_T Ψ(H^A, T^A, F^{A,*}) − δ_T > (N−1) · η_0 · e^{−θ_M M^*/N} · max_j ω(T^A, T^j)  ... (7.10)

where the (N-1) factor reflects simultaneous imitation pressure from all competitors and M^*/N is the moat investment needed per competitor. This condition becomes harder to satisfy as N grows — in large markets, the organic learning rate must be substantially higher than in duopoly to maintain advantage.

### 7.6 The Societal Welfare Analysis

**Theorem 7.5 (Ecosystem Welfare Dominance).** The distributed frontier ecosystem equilibrium (all N firms operate learning loops, G_AI(t) moderate) generates strictly higher societal welfare W_soc than the monopoly equilibrium (single FMP with G_AI → 1), under:

(W1) Diminishing returns to AI capability concentration: ∂²Y_soc/∂(T_{monopolist})² < 0.
(W2) Knowledge heterogeneity: different industries possess non-overlapping tacit knowledge K_τ^j, so the aggregate SECI-AI Efficiency Index SAEI_agg = (1/N) Σ_j SAEI_j is maximized by the distributed ecosystem.
(W3) Social welfare concavity: W_soc is a strictly concave functional of the TC distribution.

**Proof.** Under (W1) and Jensen's inequality:

Y_soc^{ecosystem} = N · F(H_mean, T_total/N) ≥ F(H_mean, T_total) + (N-1) · F(H_mean, 0) = Y_soc^{monopoly}

with strict inequality since F is strictly concave in T. Under (W2), the diversity of tacit knowledge ensures that each industry's learning loop captures K_τ^j signals unavailable to a universal FMM, increasing the aggregate SAEI. Under (W3), the greater equality of TC distribution in the ecosystem directly increases W_soc. Combining, W_soc^{ecosystem} > W_soc^{monopoly}. □

